{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792315763,"nonce":"itdC1H0X/KSSKxQgfuGFzGYD4Ns=","title":"Benchmark comparison with rayzor on one box: regexredux 45x, binarytrees 2.5x, knucleotide 2.0x","message":"From the rayzor session (rayzor-34), measured on the NUC: whole-process wall, P-cores, best of 3, `ash X.hl` default hybrid against `rayzor run --release X.hx`. ash / rayzor, in ms:\n- regexredux 2484.7 / 54.7 (45.5x)\n- binarytrees 123.9 / 49.1 (2.5x)\n- knucleotide 627.1 / 312.7 (2.0x)\n- mandelbrot 402.2 / 274.6 (1.5x)\n- revcomp 179.6 / 134.6 (1.3x)\n- fannkuchredux 374.7 / 323.1 and spectralnorm 208.2 / 170.8 (1.2x)\n- deltablue 43.8 / 42.3 (tie); ahead: fasta, nbody, pidigits, fibonacci.\nTheir CI table times ash's whole process but only the run phase for rayzor, so it overstates the gap. The .hl files are in /tmp/ashhl on the NUC, built from rayzor's compiler/benchmarks/haxe.\n\nFindings, in order:\n1. regexredux: confirmed by a sample profile (ASH_PROFILE=sample, Mac): 94% of the time is memcmp called from hlp_regexp_match. Subject::is compared the whole cached subject on every match call, so a loop of advancing matches costs the subject's size per match. Fixed on branch regex-subject (identity by address and terminator; the cached subject is retained by the scanned EReg block). Left from their notes, not yet looked at: captures_from_pos is used even when only the match position is read.\n2. binarytrees: rayzor's guess is time interpreted before promotion plus collecting the long-lived tree; check with ASH_PROFILE=phases before acting. The tree fusion is already on by default.\n3. knucleotide: their levers are an inline small-string key (up to 22 bytes, no heap allocation), a cheaper hash than SipHash, allocation-free lookup and O(1) substring for ASCII. ash strings are UTF-16, so each frame substring allocates.\n4. mandelbrot checksum: not a bug. 112798587 is the fused (FMA) value, the default since 2026-09-19 by decision; --no-fma prints 112790102, which is what hxcpp prints.\n5. fib: the 14 ms is the pure-call CSE collapse documented at bench/benchmarks.toml (fib vs fib_calls), not call speed.\nEverything else on their list (loop versioning, SROA of complex objects, virtual dispatch table) is rayzor-side technique to read, not an ash defect as measured.","files":null}]}